("- Whether the overlay is dropped when the Action's *promise* settles or when the resulting state update commits ⚠ *(the documented behavior is tied to the Action finishing; ensure your real state update is part of the same transition so the UI doesn't flash the old base state between)*.","- The overlay is tied to the Action finishing (the transition ending), not to your `setState` call: it was still applied while a post-`await` plain `setState` rendered (see §3.2). Ensure your real state update is part of the same transition."),
("(after an `await`, updates are not automatically part of the transition ⚠ verify the current rule in the `useTransition`/Actions docs), React may render the moment the Action ends with the *old* base state (overlay gone, real state not yet updated) and then render again with the new state: a **flash of the pre-mutation UI**. The documented fix for client-state versions is to wrap post-`await` state updates in `startTransition` ⚠ verify; frameworks",
 "(after an `await`, updates are not automatically part of the transition; the react.dev `useTransition` reference still says they \"currently require wrapping any set functions after the await in an additional `startTransition`\"), the update is committed on its own, outside the Action. Observed on React 19.3 with the §5.2 component: with `startTransition` around the post-`await` update the renders were `hi*` (overlay) then `hi` (confirmed), clean; with a plain post-`await` `setState`, there was an intermediate render showing **both** the confirmed row and the still-applied overlay row (`hi, hi*` — a duplicate, and duplicate React keys if both share `clientKey`) before the overlay dropped. If instead the real update commits *after* the Action ends, you get the opposite glitch, a flash of the pre-mutation base state. Either way the fix is the same: wrap post-`await` state updates in `startTransition`. Frameworks"),
("⚠ In TanStack Query v5 the relevant calls are","In TanStack Query v5 the relevant calls are"),
("(no cache write, so no rollback is needed) ⚠ *verify both approaches in the \"Optimistic Updates\" guide; names and recommended patterns have evolved across major versions.*","(no cache write, so no rollback is needed); both approaches are described in TanStack's \"Optimistic Updates\" guide, and the calls above were executed against `@tanstack/query-core` 5.104. In 5.104, callbacks are `onMutate(variables, context)`, `onSuccess(data, variables, onMutateResult, context)`, `onError(error, variables, onMutateResult, context)` and `onSettled(data, error, variables, onMutateResult, context)`, where `context` is `{ client, meta, mutationKey }`; the value you return from `onMutate` arrives as the third parameter (older v5 releases had it as `context`), so the lesson's `(err, vars, ctx)` shape still works."),
